WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.068
Build a cost estimate from the resource lifecycle, not the advertised GPU rate.

00:00:06.068 --> 00:00:17.577
Compute charges accumulate while an instance is running, including time spent installing software, downloading models, waiting for a user, or sitting idle after a job completes.

00:00:17.577 --> 00:00:25.319
Persistent disks, snapshots, object storage, reserved addresses, and logs may continue to cost money after compute stops.

00:00:25.319 --> 00:00:32.643
Data transfer charges can appear when models, datasets, or results cross regions or leave the provider.

00:00:32.643 --> 00:00:36.619
On-demand capacity offers flexibility with no long commitment.

00:00:36.619 --> 00:00:44.779
Reserved or committed capacity may reduce unit price for predictable use but transfers utilization risk to the customer.

00:00:44.779 --> 00:00:51.685
Spot or interruptible capacity can be economical for restartable batch jobs, experiments, and checkpointed training.

00:00:51.685 --> 00:00:59.845
It is a poor fit for work that cannot tolerate termination or for an interactive service without failover.

00:00:59.845 --> 00:01:04.240
Compare effective completed-work cost, not only the hourly discount.

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:08.215
Shutdown discipline is the most direct cost control.

00:01:08.215 --> 00:01:12.191
Tag resources with owner, project, purpose, and expiration.

00:01:12.191 --> 00:01:21.189
Use automatic idle detection carefully, scheduled shutdowns for development environments, and job automation that releases compute after success or failure.

00:01:21.189 --> 00:01:27.257
Distinguish stopping from deleting: a stopped instance may retain billable disks and addresses.

00:01:27.257 --> 00:01:33.325
Define retention needs before cleanup so cost control does not destroy required data.

00:01:33.325 --> 00:01:43.578
Set budgets and alerts at account and project levels, but do not treat alerts as hard limits unless the platform explicitly enforces them.

00:01:43.578 --> 00:01:49.647
Review actual charges against estimates and investigate unusual runtime, transfer, or storage growth.

00:01:49.647 --> 00:01:55.296
Record cost per completed job or per useful request alongside technical performance.

00:01:55.296 --> 00:02:03.876
The cheapest GPU hour is not economical if setup overhead, interruptions, or poor model fit require many more hours.
